---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro'; import { generateCircle } from '../core/geometry';
import CircleMiniPreview from '../components/CircleMiniPreview.astro';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '../lib/schema';
const sizes=[5,7,9,11,15,21,31,41,51,64,75,100];
const rows=sizes.map(size=>{const thin=generateCircle(size,'thin',1);const filled=generateCircle(size,'filled',1);return{size,thin:thin.blockCount,filled:filled.blockCount}});
const title='Minecraft Circle Chart – Block Templates from 5×5 to 100×100 | VoxelCurve';
const description='Use this Minecraft circle chart for common block-circle sizes from 5×5 to 100×100, with outline counts, filled counts and direct links to editable blueprints.';
const schemas=[breadcrumbSchema([{name:'VoxelCurve',url:'https://voxelcurve.com/'},{name:'Minecraft Circle Chart',url:'https://voxelcurve.com/minecraft-circle-chart'}])];
---
<BaseLayout {title} {description} canonical="https://voxelcurve.com/minecraft-circle-chart" {schemas}>
<section class="resource-hero"><div class="container"><div class="resource-hero-grid"><div><span class="eyebrow">Quick reference</span><h1>Minecraft Circle Chart</h1><p>Common Minecraft circle templates with block counts and one-click links to the interactive generator.</p></div><div class="resource-meta"><span>12 PRESETS</span><strong>5×5 → 100×100</strong><small>Outline + filled counts</small></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section resource-table-section"><div class="container"><div class="section-kicker">01 / Circle presets</div><div class="table-wrap blueprint-table"><table><caption>Minecraft circles chart: outside dimensions and block counts</caption><thead><tr><th>Preview</th><th>Size</th><th>Outline blocks</th><th>Filled blocks</th><th>Open blueprint</th></tr></thead><tbody>{rows.map(r=><tr><td><CircleMiniPreview size={r.size} /></td><td><strong>{r.size}×{r.size}</strong></td><td>{r.thin}</td><td>{r.filled}</td><td><a class="text-link" href={`/#c-${r.size}-thin-1`}>Open {r.size}×{r.size} →</a></td></tr>)}</tbody></table></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container"><div class="section-kicker">02 / Choose a scale</div><div class="section-split"><div><h2>Which circle size should you use?</h2><p class="section-intro">Think in terms of footprint first, then use the exact blueprint when you are ready to place blocks.</p></div><div class="capability-list"><article><span>SM</span><div><h3>5–15 blocks</h3><p>Wells, compact towers, tree bases and decorative circular details.</p></div></article><article><span>MD</span><div><h3>21–41 blocks</h3><p>Towers, rooms, arenas, observatories and dome foundations.</p></div></article><article><span>LG</span><div><h3>51–100 blocks</h3><p>Large builds where guided segments eliminate costly recounting.</p></div></article></div></div></div></section>
<section class="section"><div class="container editorial-grid"><aside class="editorial-aside"><div class="section-kicker">03 / Reading the chart</div><p>Use the preset table for quick sizing, then move into the generator when you need guided placement.</p></aside><div class="prose editorial-prose"><h2>How to read a Minecraft circle chart</h2><p>Every chart size is the outside width of the circle in blocks. Odd sizes have one center block; even sizes are centered between four blocks. The outline count is the number of blocks in a one-block-thick boundary, while the filled count includes the entire disk.</p><p>This page also works as a Minecraft circle diagram, circle template and circle grid reference: each preset is rendered from the same geometry used by the interactive generator rather than from a separate hand-drawn image.</p><h2>Need a different size?</h2><p>The chart is a shortcut, not a limit. The <a class="text-link" href="/">Minecraft Circle Generator</a> supports custom diameters and produces exact row and segment instructions for the size you need.</p></div></div></section>
<section class="section related-section"><div class="container">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Choose a template for your build</h2>
    <h3>Which Minecraft circle template fits a small tower?</h3>
    <p>Start with 11×11 for a compact tower or 21×21 when you need more interior space. If you need a “circle template Minecraft” reference, choose the full outside width rather than the interior room width. Open the preset for instructions that show exactly where each row begins.</p>
    <h3>How do I read the Minecraft circle graph?</h3>
    <p>Each occupied cell in the Minecraft circle grid represents a block. The preview shows an outline; the two count columns distinguish that wall perimeter from a filled floor. Open the blueprint for segment lengths instead of estimating them from the small image.</p>
    <h3>What if my diameter is not in the table?</h3>
    <p>The table covers 12 common footprints. A “circle chart Minecraft” search is useful for comparing those sizes, but a fixed table cannot show every diameter. Open the generator and enter an outside width from 3 to 512 blocks for a custom blueprint.</p>
    <p>Use this circle chart for Minecraft as a sizing reference before gathering materials. Once you choose a footprint, keep its editable blueprint open during construction.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="section-kicker">04 / Next step</div>
  <h2>Turn the chart into a build plan</h2>
  <div class="related-list">
    <a href="/"><span class="related-index">01</span><div><h3>Circle Generator</h3><p>Use any custom diameter and get exact guided row segments.</p></div><span class="related-arrow">↗</span></a>
    <a href="/how-to-make-a-circle-in-minecraft"><span class="related-index">02</span><div><h3>Circle Building Guide</h3><p>Learn centers, anchors, symmetry and large-circle construction.</p></div><span class="related-arrow">↗</span></a>
    <a href="/minecraft-dome-generator"><span class="related-index">03</span><div><h3>Dome Generator</h3><p>Use a circular footprint as the base for a layer-by-layer dome.</p></div><span class="related-arrow">↗</span></a>
  </div>
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
